Been playing for a few months, then decided to learn one class after
noodling around and ascend with it. That first month, kept restarting
to get good starting stats, spells, and items, then decided that
wasn't really the point and played every new character like he was
going to make it. Didn't really expect to ascend this guy; it just
worked out that way.

Played for three days (61,000 turns). Got a mixed break with a bones
file (co-aligned priest in Minetown, where bones were - my cache of
spellbooks in a secured room nearby was uncursed and ready for use; on
the other hand, that Minetown only had two shops - delicatessen and
lighting - so useless otherwise). Went polyless, genoless, used two
wishes (one for scrolls of charging), only scribed Elbereth once to
secure the Candle, Book, and Bell, and carried out every gem I'd
found.

The game started off inauspiciously, with my cat Morris getting beaned
with a rock by the second level; initial gear was the usual stuff, no
marker or blindfold, spells were Force Bolt and Light, except that I
had a +5 Ring on Gain Constitution - made a real difference over time.
Only when I hit the third level and found three altars, two of them
co-aligned, dig I suspect that old Thobalt might actually make it out
feet-first. An orgy of killing ("terminal spiritual uplift") brought
me Magicbane and Frost Brand fairly early, but I wasn't crowned until
about level 16 (already had all of the resistances crowning grants).
Found a luckstone prior to Sokoban (was an amulet of reflection there,
anyway), and a bag of holding in a shop around Dlvl5. There was an
overpriced wand there, too, and I started to get very excited - but I
had no way to identify it at that time, and was pretty broke (no pet,
remember?), so had to wait until I tackled Minetown to buy it. Wand of
Death, actually, but nice to have. 

While wandering around I found a magic marker, a cornuthaum, and
lenses - first time one of my wizards actually dressed like a wizard
("See the pointy hat? A pointy hat, a wizard." - Rincewind). Then
found a +4 leather armor that served until I reached Fort Ludios and
wished for SDSM. That was the only thing I wished for, aside from
scrolls of charging…had three wishes ready to go, a blessed scroll of
genocide, and (finally) a spellbook of polymorph, but held off to see
if I could ascend the hard(er) way.

Scribed Elbereth once, on the Vibrating Square, to secure the Bell,
Book, and Candle. Recalled later that I had a scroll of scare monster,
so that wasn't even necessary. Big luck was good (items found, some
lower levels had stairs extremely close together), little luck was bad
(Rodney 11 times?!? almost every blessed enchant did the minimum, the
one durable thing I wished for (SDSM) arrived +0, and of course I
fought all three Riders before finding the right altar).

Did no nurse-dancing, succubus-baiting (but didn't pass them up on the
way through, though: had a quickie with Rodney hot in pursuit - "an
elf's gotta do what an elf's gotta do!"), or even shoplifting (pet cat
fatality aided ethics). Found the amulet of life-saving at Vlad's, and
strapped it on immediately.

Made a couple of annoying mistakes: hoarded my magic markers until I
decided what spells I'd need, then wrote those books. Only problem
was, forgot which Marker I was holding and wasted 42 charges trying to
write a sixth-level spell (the other Marker had 84 charges, and was
the intended instrument). Did the Elvish jig of irritation. Got killed
the only time running with scissors (fell in a pit with a wielded
cockatrice on the way to Rodney). Did the Elvish jig of irritation
again, and noted that I'd done the dance enough to move up a skill
slot ("You feel that you could be more irritated!").
 
Reflected on the last time I'd gotten this far (and suffered
back-to-back cockatrice-related fatalities), and put the damned thing
in a bag until I was actually fighting, then checked that gloves were
on, issued thwappage, then put it away. One actually decayed
mid-fight, and I ended up going hand-to-tentacle with a master mind
flayer. Approximately fifteen lost dungeon levels later, I realized
what was going on and gave him the finger (of death) and restored
myself. Then I teleported to the top and walked all of the way back,
restoring levels. Had teleport control and magic mapping, so wasn't
unduly tedious.

This was the first time I'd made it to the Wizard, and the rest of the
game was all new. Found a spellbook of cancellation, built my levels,
and then threw away my wands of cancellation. Likewise cached my spare
bag of holding, and the possibility of putting A inside of B. Hadn't
seen how the ending screen went, so didn't think to save list of stuff
I carried (a pity); got screen captures or text pastes of the rest.
Learned that potions of (un)holy water can be used like sourdough
starter - hadn't occurred to me before.

This game felt different, too: I played more carefully and saved my
goodies (three potions of gain level, three wishes, several potions of
full healing), and brought all of my gems out and a good chunk of the
gold (went through all of the dungeon branches). Early enchant armor
scrolls went to erosion-proofing instead of AC, so my AC was poor for
quite a while, but everything I intended to finish with I finished
with. Selected spells needed, used markers very carefully and wrote
books I'd need later instead of writing scrolls. Likewise with
experience slots - cast a lot of unnecessary spells to build skills
early on for areas I'd want later (matter, for example). Didn't use
all of the slots immediately, and only built two skills to Expert -
Attack and Matter spells (for Finger of Death and Cancellation).
Should be easier next time, knowing what to expect.


